  "house_popular_vote_margin": null,
  "note": "Filled on election night: national House popular-vote margin (R minus D), used to separate the national swing from race-specific effects."
 },
 "method": "Each race's result margin (Republican minus Democratic, in points) is compared with the same state's or district's 2024 presidential margin. The difference is the swing. The test pre-registered here: if MAHA backing adds turnout or persuasion, races with sourced MAHA backing should show a less negative (or more positive) swing than comparable races without it, after subtracting the national swing. 7 MAHA-backed races (two statewide, five House) against 16 without is a small sample; the overlay will say what it can and cannot support.",
 "pre_registered": [
  "Primary comparison: mean swing (result margin minus 2024 presidential margin) in MAHA-backed races vs. non-backed races in the same office tier (statewide; House).",
  "Control for national environment by subtracting the national House popular-vote swing from 2024 (Trump +1.5 nationally in 2024 presidential; House 2024 R +2.6).",
  "Iowa is the single best test: the only statewide nominee with MAHA PAC backing (Lahn) running alongside a Senate nominee without it (Hinson) in the same electorate on the same day. The Lahn\u2013Hinson gap is MAHA's measurable effect in Iowa, net of candidate quality.",
  "Turnout check: Iowa, Michigan and Georgia total votes vs 2022 and vs the 2024-to-2022 drop-off shown on the race-level table; a smaller midterm drop-off in counties with high MAHA search interest would support the turnout thesis.",
  "What would falsify the turnout thesis: MAHA-backed candidates underperform the 2024 presidential baseline by more than non-backed Republicans in the same tier, and Iowa turnout drop-off is no smaller than 2022's."
 ],
